apiVersion: meta.pkg.crossplane.io/v1
kind: Provider
metadata:
  name: provider-{{providerName}}
  annotations:
    # NOTE: this block IS the Marketplace listing. Everything here is rendered
    # publicly, so keep it accurate.
    meta.crossplane.io/maintainer: {{gitUserId}} (https://{{gitHost}}/{{gitUserId}})
    meta.crossplane.io/source: {{gitHost}}/{{gitUserId}}/{{gitRepoId}}
    meta.crossplane.io/license: Apache-2.0
    meta.crossplane.io/description: |
      Manage {{providerTitle}} as Kubernetes resources.
    meta.crossplane.io/readme: |
      `provider-{{providerName}}` manages {{providerTitle}} as Kubernetes resources, so
      phone numbers, messaging services, SIP domains and the rest can be
      declared alongside the rest of your cluster and reconciled by
      Crossplane.

      It is GENERATED, and not by a generator written for it: the Kinds below
      come from [twilio-oai](https://github.com/twilio/twilio-oai), {{providerTitle}}'s
      own API description, through
      [twilio-oai-generator](https://github.com/twilio/twilio-oai-generator),
      {{providerTitle}}'s own openapi-generator, with a Crossplane generator added
      alongside its Terraform one. The API client is
      [twilio-go](https://github.com/twilio/twilio-go).

      So what this provider can manage is what {{providerTitle}} describes, and which
      operations make up a resource is {{providerTitle}}'s own answer rather than a
      guess -- the same answer its Terraform provider gets, from the same
      code.

      ## Managed resources
{{#apiInfo}}{{#apis}}{{#operations}}

      * `{{kind}}`
{{/operations}}{{/apis}}{{/apiInfo}}

      ## Getting started

      Install the provider, then give it a credential. {{providerTitle}} authenticates
      with HTTP basic, and there are two things to put in the secret -- an
      Account SID with its Auth Token, or an API Key with its secret. Prefer
      the second: a key can be revoked without rotating the account.

      There is no endpoint to set. Every operation's base URL comes from the
      SDK, and the API description gives a different one per product.

      ```yaml
      apiVersion: pkg.crossplane.io/v1
      kind: Provider
      metadata:
        name: provider-{{providerName}}
      spec:
        package: xpkg.upbound.io/n-at-han-k/provider-{{providerName}}:v0.1.0
      ---
      apiVersion: v1
      kind: Secret
      metadata:
        name: {{providerName}}-creds
        namespace: crossplane-system
      type: Opaque
      stringData:
        credentials: |
          {
            "accountSid": "ACxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx",
            "apiKey":     "SKxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx",
            "apiSecret":  "..."
          }
      ---
      apiVersion: {{groupName}}/{{crdVersion}}
      kind: ClusterProviderConfig
      metadata:
        name: default
      spec:
        credentials:
          source: Secret
          secretRef:
            namespace: crossplane-system
            name: {{providerName}}-creds
            key: credentials
      ```

      `authToken` is accepted in place of `apiKey`/`apiSecret`, and
      `username`/`password` as aliases for whichever pair is in use.
      `edge` and `region` reach {{providerTitle}} somewhere other than the default.

      A field is spelled the way {{providerTitle}} takes it -- `spec.forProvider.FriendlyName`,
      not `friendlyName` -- because the wire name IS the field name, so there
      is no translation layer for anything to drift out of.
      `status.atProvider` spells the same values the way {{providerTitle}} answers them,
      in snake_case.

      See the `examples/` directory in the source repository for a manifest per
      managed resource.
spec:
  # SafeStart, NOT "safe-start". crossplane/provider-template ships the
  # kebab-case spelling, which is not the enum value: Crossplane silently
  # prunes it, the ProviderRevision comes up with no capabilities, and the RBAC
  # manager therefore never grants the CRD get/list/watch that a safe-start
  # provider needs. The binary still runs its crd-gate, which then cannot sync
  # and the container CrashLoopBackOffs with "failed to wait for crd-gate
  # caches to sync".
  capabilities:
    - SafeStart
